Falsifies `nifefs::verification::a_short_image_is_refused_not_indexed`.

The defect: the magic check moved ahead of the length guard. It reads as the cheaper rejection,
since most images a server is handed that are wrong are not nifefs images at all, and "is this
ours" before "is this complete" is the order plenty of parsers use. What it gives up is the
harness's whole claim: parse now reads the image before it has measured it. On an image shorter
than the magic, `image[0..8]` panics, which is a filesystem server brought down by an image a
client handed it; on any other short image the verdict depends on bytes nobody should have read.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_eq!(Fs::parse(&image).err(), Some(Error::Truncated))`,
because a short image whose first eight bytes are not the magic now comes back `BadMagic`. Red in
0.79 s of verification (2.5 s wall, crate already built) on 2026-09-24.

**Replaces the record #1156 landed, which never reached a verdict.** That one weakened the guard to
`image.len() < BLOCK`, a more realistic defect, and CI cancelled its replay at the 45-minute job
timeout (run 35953923275). The reason is the harness's shape, not the solver: any guard weak
enough to admit the harness's one concrete length lets symbolic execution into `parse`'s entry loop,
whose bound is a `count` read from symbolic bytes, and with no unwind bound CBMC unrolls it without
end. An off-by-one at the exact boundary (`< DIR_BLOCKS * BLOCK - 1`) was tried first, as the
smaller mutation, and hung the same way (killed at 420 s with no verdict). This mutation keeps the
length check concrete, so the loop stays unreachable and the solver answers at once. The harness's
own comment records what that leaves uncaught.

